# decision Manifest dependency edits preserve human YAML

Changes to `dependencies` in an existing `a2amodule.yml` go through `yaml.Node`, not a
struct-wide marshal. Existing dependency nodes are updated in place and unrelated comments,
scalar styles, flow collections, ordering, and extension nodes survive. A full marshal is for
new manifests and locks; `fmt` canonicalizes through the YAML node tree.

Adapter transactions snapshot both a generated integration file and its precisely marked root
include/apply line. Gradle therefore adds both settings DSL variants and both
`deps/git-a2a.settings.gradle(.kts)` outputs to the same rollback set as CMake.
MSBuild root project names are discovered dynamically for staging and rollback because their
names are project-defined; `deps/git-a2a.targets` remains a fixed generated output.
Maven snapshots both the root `pom.xml` managed blocks and its tool-owned generated reactor POM.
Meson snapshots both root `meson.build` and its tool-owned `deps/git-a2a/meson.build` include.

# invariant Dependency mutations are recoverable

`add`, each dependency processed by `update`, and `set` stage fetched manifests, cards, and
surfaces outside the live cache. They snapshot every package-manager file they can modify,
write manifest and lock atomically, and only then replace the live cache by rename. Any wiring,
metadata, or cache replacement failure restores package-manager files and the previous
manifest/lock/cache; a command must not report success over a partial mutation.
When a dependency requires signed commits, verification is part of that staging gate and runs
before any durable or adapter write. The `allowed_signers` path is repository-relative after
both lexical and symlink resolution; an escape outside the consumer repository is rejected.
An explicit signer bypass is recorded as `verified: skipped`, never presented as verification.
A rejected signed-commit check reports Git's signature status, signer, key identifier, and
fingerprint; absent values are printed as `none`, so a trust failure identifies the credential
that was rejected without implying that an unsigned object had one.

# decision Standalone Windows upgrades replace around the running executable

Windows cannot overwrite a running executable. The binary-channel `upgrade` first renames the
current executable to the adjacent `.old` path, installs the verified replacement, restores the
old executable if installation fails, and removes a leftover `.old` best-effort on the next
invocation. Package-manager channels never use self-upgrade.

<system_invariant name="Lock hashes describe Git blobs, not worktree translations" id="blob_bytes">

Fetched manifest and card bytes used for hashes are read from Git objects (`git archive` or `git
show <commit>:<path>`), never from a checked-out worktree. In particular, Windows
`core.autocrlf` must not turn repository LF bytes into CRLF before lock verification. A regression
test enables `core.autocrlf=true` while restoring a locked local-bare dependency.

</system_invariant>

# test Internal mutation invariants

- YAML dependency edits retain comments, folded scalars, flow collections, and `x-` fields.
- A failed `add`, `update`, or `set` leaves manifest, lock, cache, and adapter files unchanged.
- Windows replacement restores the old executable after a failed install.

# test Polyglot lifecycle stays atomic across every registered adapter

The local-bare-repository end-to-end scenario runs `add → sync → status → update → set → pin →
unpin → remove` with every supported native Git ecosystem detected at once. It verifies one
lock commit in every project file, clean offline status, preservation of human manifest text,
and complete unwiring at the end. Adding an adapter includes it in this scenario in the same
commit.
